# Display names for the entities added after the first roster pass.
#
# ⚠️ These live here rather than in twilightforest.lang only because that file is owned by another
# pass and two writers on one file is how a merge loses a line. BTA loads every .lang file in this
# directory and merges them, so the split has no effect at runtime -- a key defined here is
# indistinguishable from one defined there.
#
# The key shape is BTA 8.0's: mob names resolve through the guidebook namespace as
#     guidebook.section.mob.<entity id>.name
# where <entity id> is exactly the string TFEntities.register() passes. A mismatch shows the raw key
# in the world rather than falling back to anything.

# The Lich. "Twilight Lich" is upstream's own name for it, not an embellishment.
guidebook.section.mob.lich.name=Twilight Lich

# The Lich's bolt. Registered as an entity because the id is what the spawn packet carries; it is
# never named in the UI, but an unnamed registration shows the key in death messages and F3.
guidebook.section.mob.lichbolt.name=Lich Bolt

# The firefly spark thrown off by a firefly. Nothing spawns it yet -- see EntityTFTinyFirefly.
guidebook.section.mob.tinyfirefly.name=Firefly

# The two bosses that finish the mod.
#
# ⚠️ "Minoshroom" is upstream's own name and is not a typo for Minotaur -- it is a minotaur crossed
# with a mooshroom, which is what the skin, the mushroom houses around it and the bowl of stew it
# drops are all saying. The entity ID is `minoshroom`; its SKIN is minoshroomtaur.png, which is a
# different string and belongs in asset-bridge.properties, not here.
guidebook.section.mob.minoshroom.name=Minoshroom

# ⚠️ "Questing Ram", not "Quest Ram". Upstream's class is EntityTFQuestRam and its display name is
# the longer form; the id here has to stay `questram` because that is what TFEntities registers and
# what a saved world holds.
guidebook.section.mob.questram.name=Questing Ram

# --- Sound subtitles ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The strings shown when subtitles are on, keyed by whatever `subtitle` a sound event declares in
# assets/twilightforest/sounds/sounds.json.
#
# ⚠️ This is the port's own layer, not Benimatic's. Minecraft 1.4.7 had no subtitles at all, so there
# is nothing upstream to be faithful to and nothing in the archive to bridge -- the key names and the
# wording are both decisions taken here.
#
# ONE KEY PER MOB, not one per event, which is the convention sounds.json already set for the raven
# (caw and squawk share a key) and the kobold (mutter, hurt and die share one). So the Naga's key has
# to cover its hiss, its rattle AND its hurt, and the wording below is loose enough to.
subtitles.twilightforest.naga=Naga hisses
subtitles.twilightforest.redcap=Redcap mutters
subtitles.twilightforest.wraith=Wraith moans
